IMPORT prelude

-- Mental Health (Care and Treatment) Act 2008 (Singapore) -- police apprehension (s 7), refusing to produce a person for police
-- inspection (s 8(3), (4)), the ladder of involuntary detention orders (ss 10, 13), who may not sign an order (s 11), discharge
-- (ss 12, 14), leave of absence and retaking (s 15), offences against patients (s 22), protection of persons acting under the Act
-- (s 25), improper reception or detention (s 26), maximum penalties (ss 8(4), 22(7), 26(1), 32(3)) and composition (s 30).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/MHCTA2008.txt. The deposit
-- labels itself the 2020 Revised Edition incorporating amendments up to 1 December 2021, and shows later amendments to Act 16 of
-- 2024 (wef 1 January 2025, s 7). Its metadata says "Current version as at 01 Oct 2026".
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 6 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to the rules a police officer, doctor,
-- carer or institution actually meets: when a person must be apprehended, how long each detention order lasts and what the next one
-- needs, who may sign, discharge and leave, and the offences. Not encoded: designation and management of institutions (ss 3-5),
-- visitors' inspections, transfer and removal from Singapore (ss 16-19), maintenance orders (ss 20, 21, 27, 28), the evidential
-- effect of orders (s 24), rules (s 32 beyond its penalty cap), the Schedule forms beyond what s 10 says about them.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Mental Health (Care and Treatment) Act 2008 -- detention`

§§ `7 -- apprehension by the police`

-- s 7(1): "It is the duty of every police officer or special police officer to apprehend any person ("P") where the police officer
-- or special police officer believes that P may endanger P's or any other person's life or personal safety, and P's conduct is
-- reasonably suspected to be attributable to a mental disorder", and take P "without delay" to a medical practitioner (s 9) or a
-- designated medical practitioner at a psychiatric institution (s 10).
-- s 7(2)(a) [Act 16 of 2024]: a reasonable belief that P is endangering P's OWN life or safety "is sufficient basis" for the suspicion
-- that P's conduct is attributable to a mental disorder. (aa): it is sufficient that the danger is "only reasonably likely to occur
-- and need not be imminent, and actual harm is not required".
-- Imminence and actual harm are therefore not inputs: they cannot change the answer.
GIVEN `danger is reasonably likely` IS A BOOLEAN, `danger to P's own life or safety` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `danger to another person's life or safety` IS A BOOLEAN, `conduct suspected to stem from mental disorder` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer must apprehend P` IF
        `danger is reasonably likely`
    AND (`danger to P's own life or safety` OR `danger to another person's life or safety`)
    AND (`conduct suspected to stem from mental disorder` OR `danger to P's own life or safety`)

§§ `8(3), (4) -- producing P for police inspection`

-- s 8(3): a police officer "not below the rank of sergeant" may visit a person supposed to be mentally disordered in the care of a
-- relative or other person, to see whether P is properly cared for or ill-treated or neglected.
-- s 8(4): that relative or other person "is legally bound to produce P for the inspection of the police officer" and on refusal is
-- guilty of an offence, fine up to $4,000.
-- Inference: the duty is owed to an officer visiting under subsection (3), so a refusal to an officer below sergeant is not encoded
-- as the offence.
GIVEN `has charge of P` IS A BOOLEAN, `the officer is sergeant or above` IS A BOOLEAN, `refused to produce P` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `refusing to produce P is an offence` IF
    `has charge of P` AND `the officer is sergeant or above` AND `refused to produce P`

§§ `10 and 13 -- the ladder of detention orders`

-- s 10(1): a designated medical practitioner (DMP) may sign a Form 1 order; the person "may be detained for a period of 72 hours".
-- s 10(2): a further "one month" if, before the 72 hours end, "another designated medical practitioner" examines the patient, is of
-- the opinion that further treatment is required, and signs Form 2.
-- s 10(3)-(5): no further detention unless, before the month ends, the patient is brought before "2 designated medical practitioners
-- ... one of whom must be a psychiatrist, who have examined the patient separately and who are both satisfied"; each signs Form 3;
-- two such orders authorise detention "for a period not exceeding 6 months".
-- s 13(1)-(3), (6): the visitors must apply (Form 4) to a Magistrate for a patient detained under s 10(3) who should be further
-- detained; the Magistrate's order (Form 5) authorises "a period not exceeding 12 months"; further applications may be made.
DECLARE Order IS ONE OF
    `a Form 1 order`
    `a Form 2 order`
    `two Form 3 orders`
    `a Magistrate's order`

GIVEN `the order` IS AN Order
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `the longest detention authorised by` IS
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `a Form 1 order` THEN "72 hours"
    WHEN `a Form 2 order` THEN "1 month"
    WHEN `two Form 3 orders` THEN "6 months"
    WHEN `a Magistrate's order` THEN "12 months"

GIVEN `the order` IS AN Order
GIVETH AN Order
DECIDE `the next order needed after` IS
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `a Form 1 order` THEN `a Form 2 order`
    WHEN `a Form 2 order` THEN `two Form 3 orders`
    WHEN `two Form 3 orders` THEN `a Magistrate's order`
    WHEN `a Magistrate's order` THEN `a Magistrate's order`

GIVEN `examined before the 72 hours end` IS A BOOLEAN, `by a different DMP` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `of opinion further treatment needed` IS A BOOLEAN, `the signer is barred by s 11` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Form 2 order is valid` IF
        `examined before the 72 hours end` AND `by a different DMP` AND `of opinion further treatment needed`
    AND (NOT `the signer is barred by s 11`)

GIVEN `seen before the month ends` IS A BOOLEAN, `number of DMPs` IS A NUMBER, `one is a psychiatrist` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `examined separately` IS A BOOLEAN, `all satisfied further treatment needed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Form 3 orders are valid` IF
        `seen before the month ends` AND `number of DMPs` AT LEAST 2 AND `one is a psychiatrist`
    AND `examined separately` AND `all satisfied further treatment needed`

-- s 10(6): "A person must not be detained at a psychiatric institution for treatment unless -- (a) the person is suffering from a
-- mental disorder which warrants the detention ... and (b) it is necessary in the interests of the health or safety of the person or
-- for the protection of other persons".
GIVEN `a mental disorder warranting detention` IS A BOOLEAN, `necessary for the person's health or safety` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `necessary to protect others` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `detention is permitted` IF
    `a mental disorder warranting detention` AND (`necessary for the person's health or safety` OR `necessary to protect others`)

§§ `11 -- who must not sign an order`

-- s 11: a medical practitioner who "(a) is the husband, wife, father, father-in-law, mother, mother-in-law, son, son-in-law,
-- daughter, daughter-in-law, brother, brother-in-law, sister or sister-in-law of the patient; (b) stands in a fiduciary relationship
-- to the patient; or (c) is treating the patient, must not sign any order under this Act".
-- The list is narrower than "relative" in s 2: grandparents, grandchildren, uncles, aunts, nephews and nieces are not in it.
DECLARE Relation IS ONE OF
    `no relation`
    `spouse`
    `parent`
    `parent-in-law`
    `child`
    `child-in-law`
    `sibling`
    `sibling-in-law`
    `grandparent`
    `grandchild`
    `uncle or aunt`
    `nephew or niece`
    `cousin`

GIVEN `the relation` IS A Relation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the relation bars signing` IF
    CONSIDER `the relation`
    WHEN `spouse` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `parent` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `parent-in-law` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `child` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `child-in-law` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `sibling` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `sibling-in-law` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the relation` IS A Relation, `in a fiduciary relationship` IS A BOOLEAN, `is treating the patient` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the practitioner may sign an order` IF
    (NOT `the relation bars signing` `the relation`) AND (NOT `in a fiduciary relationship`) AND (NOT `is treating the patient`)

§§ `12 and 14 -- discharge`

-- s 12: "the principal officer or a psychiatrist of a psychiatric institution may by writing under his or her hand order the
-- discharge of any patient". s 14: "2 of the visitors ... of whom one must be a medical practitioner" may order the discharge.
GIVEN `signed by the principal officer` IS A BOOLEAN, `signed by a psychiatrist of the institution` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `visitors signing` IS A NUMBER, `a signing visitor is a medical practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the discharge is validly ordered` IF
        `signed by the principal officer`
    OR  `signed by a psychiatrist of the institution`
    OR  (`visitors signing` AT LEAST 2 AND `a signing visitor is a medical practitioner`)

§§ `15 -- leave of absence and retaking`

-- s 15(1): two visitors, on the principal officer's written recommendation, may permit absence "for as long as they think fit".
-- (2): a certificate of 2 DMPs before the end of the absence that detention is no longer necessary -- the patient "is deemed to be
-- discharged". (3): if the patient does not return "within 28 days of the end of the period of absence", and no certificate has been
-- received, the patient "may be retaken as in the case of an escape".
-- (4): the principal officer may permit absence "for any period not exceeding 6 months", and if "upon the end of the period of
-- absence the patient does not return", the patient may be retaken. No 28-day grace is given under (4).
-- Inference: the (2) certificate is read as applying to absence under (1) only, as (2) and (3) are framed together.
DECLARE Leave IS ONE OF
    `leave from two visitors`
    `leave from the principal officer`

GIVEN `the leave` IS A Leave, `days overdue` IS A NUMBER, `a discharge certificate was received` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the patient may be retaken` IF
    CONSIDER `the leave`
    WHEN `leave from two visitors` THEN `days overdue` > 28 AND (NOT `a discharge certificate was received`)
    WHEN `leave from the principal officer` THEN `days overdue` > 0

GIVEN `months of leave` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the principal officer may grant the leave` IF
    `months of leave` AT MOST 6

§§ `22 -- offences against patients`

-- s 22(1): any medical practitioner, nurse, attendant or other person "employed by or rendering service in any psychiatric
-- institution or hospital mentioned in section 15(5), who ill-treats any patient" is guilty of an offence.
-- (2): ill-treatment is (a) physical or sexual abuse; (b) wilfully or unreasonably doing an act endangering safety or causing
-- unnecessary pain, emotional injury or injury to health; (c) wilfully or unreasonably neglecting the patient in such circumstances.
-- (3): neglecting to provide "adequate food, clothing, medical aid or care" is deemed such neglect.
-- (4): conviction is possible even though injury was "obviated by the action of another person", or "despite the death of the patient".
GIVEN `works in the institution or s 15(5) hospital` IS A BOOLEAN, `physical or sexual abuse` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `wilful act endangering or harming the patient` IS A BOOLEAN, `wilful neglect endangering or harming the patient` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `wilfully failed to provide food, clothing, medical aid or care` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ill-treatment of a patient is an offence` IF
        `works in the institution or s 15(5) hospital`
    AND (   `physical or sexual abuse`
         OR `wilful act endangering or harming the patient`
         OR `wilful neglect endangering or harming the patient`
         OR `wilfully failed to provide food, clothing, medical aid or care`)

-- s 22(5): "Any person who has sexual intercourse with a patient of a psychiatric institution, while in that institution, shall be
-- guilty of an offence." (6): "Consent is not a defence ... if the accused knew or had reason to suspect" the person was a patient.
-- Not limited to staff. Inference: consent is a defence only where the accused neither knew nor had reason to suspect.
GIVEN `the other person is a patient there` IS A BOOLEAN, `in the institution` IS A BOOLEAN, `the patient consented` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `knew or had reason to suspect a patient` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `sexual intercourse with a patient is an offence` IF
        `the other person is a patient there` AND `in the institution`
    AND ((NOT `the patient consented`) OR `knew or had reason to suspect a patient`)

§§ `8(4), 22(7), 26(1), 32(3) -- maximum penalties`

-- s 8(4): fine up to $4,000. s 22(7)(a)(i): ill-treatment causing death, $20,000 or 7 years or both; (ii) otherwise $5,000 or 4 years
-- or both. (b): sexual intercourse, $20,000 or 10 years or both. s 26(1): $5,000 or 3 years or both. s 32(3): rules may make a
-- contravention punishable with up to $5,000 or 6 months or both.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `refusing to produce P`
    `ill-treatment causing death`
    `ill-treatment not causing death`
    `sexual intercourse with a patient`
    `improper reception or detention`
    `breach of the rules`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the maximum fine for` IS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `refusing to produce P` THEN 4000
    WHEN `ill-treatment causing death` THEN 20000
    WHEN `ill-treatment not causing death` THEN 5000
    WHEN `sexual intercourse with a patient` THEN 20000
    WHEN `improper reception or detention` THEN 5000
    WHEN `breach of the rules` THEN 5000

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the maximum months of imprisonment for` IS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `refusing to produce P` THEN 0
    WHEN `ill-treatment causing death` THEN 84
    WHEN `ill-treatment not causing death` THEN 48
    WHEN `sexual intercourse with a patient` THEN 120
    WHEN `improper reception or detention` THEN 36
    WHEN `breach of the rules` THEN 6

§§ `25 -- protection of persons acting under the Act`

-- s 25(1): a person who did anything under the Act "shall not be liable to any civil or criminal proceedings ... unless the person
-- has acted in bad faith or without reasonable care". (2): no proceedings "without the permission of the court", and permission is
-- not given unless there is "substantial ground for the contention" of bad faith or want of reasonable care. (3): notice of the
-- application must be given to the person, who may be heard.
GIVEN `acted in bad faith` IS A BOOLEAN, `acted without reasonable care` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person acting under the Act may be liable` IF
    `acted in bad faith` OR `acted without reasonable care`

GIVEN `substantial ground shown` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice given to the person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may permit proceedings` IF
    `substantial ground shown` AND `notice given to the person`

§§ `26 -- improper reception or detention`

-- s 26(1): subject to s 25, any person who "(a) otherwise than in accordance with the provisions of this Act receives or detains in a
-- psychiatric institution a person who is or is alleged to be mentally disordered; or (b) for gain detains in any place that is not a
-- psychiatric institution, 2 or more mentally disordered persons" commits an offence. (2): prosecution only "with the consent of the
-- Public Prosecutor".
GIVEN `detained in an institution outside the Act` IS A BOOLEAN, `for gain` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the place is a psychiatric institution` IS A BOOLEAN, `mentally disordered persons detained` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `improper detention is an offence` IF
        (`the place is a psychiatric institution` AND `detained in an institution outside the Act`)
    OR  (`for gain` AND (NOT `the place is a psychiatric institution`) AND `mentally disordered persons detained` AT LEAST 2)

§§ `30 -- composition`

-- s 30(1): the Director-General or an authorised officer may compound an offence "prescribed as a compoundable offence" by collecting
-- "a sum not exceeding $2,000". (2): on payment, no further proceedings.
GIVEN `prescribed as compoundable` IS A BOOLEAN, `the sum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence may be compounded for` IF
    `prescribed as compoundable` AND `the sum` AT MOST 2000
